{- | Floor gate for the in-language (`medaka test`) `stdlib/` suite (#3214).

   It asserts that the doctests, `prop "…"` and `test "…"` declarations
   written inside `stdlib/*.mdk` are discovered and pass. Each module is
   spawned as its own `medaka test` run — twice, once per engine, wherever
   it can take both; no external oracle is needed.

   Why a roster is the only thing that runs them. A `stdlib/` module's types
   are walked whenever something imports it, but no importer runs its
   assertions, and `make medaka`, `make check-self` and
   `test/typecheck_compiler_source.sh` run none of them either: that is
   [W-MODULE-BLIND] in `AGENTS.md`, and it applies to every module below.
   Importing is not running — `pds/` imports `stdlib/hex.mdk` and exercises
   it as ordinary code while `hex.mdk`'s own out-of-domain masking pin went
   unrun, and `stdlib/bytes.mdk` stays outside the COMPILER's import closure
   by design, since that is what keeps it free of a seed re-mint, even
   though plenty of other code imports it.

   Two other vehicles reach part of `stdlib/`, and neither is a substitute
   for a roster closed over the directory: `test/diff_compiler_test_native.sh`
   spawns the three modules in its `MODULES` list, and
   `test/diff_compiler_fmt_test.mdk`'s `testReportCorpus` golden-pins the
   `medaka test` report of fifteen more. A golden pin fails when a count
   moves in either direction, so it grades a module that is already listed;
   it cannot say that a NEW module's assertions are run by nothing, which is
   what the closure check below is for.

   The anti-rot guard (`docs/ops/TESTING-DESIGN.md` §0: "this didn't run" is
   indistinguishable from "this passed"): a `medaka test` file with zero
   assertions exits 0, vacuously green. So exit 0 alone is not enough. Every
   module's executed-assertion count (`summary.passed` from `medaka test
   --json`) must meet the per-module floor committed below, the corpus total
   must be nonzero, and every `stdlib/*.mdk` must have either a `suites` row
   or a reasoned `exempt` row — a module with neither fails this gate
   rather than going uncounted.

   This module lists a directory and spawns subprocesses, so it runs under
   `medaka test --native`. `Makefile`'s `test:` target invokes it that way,
   and `test/CI-COVERAGE-TOOLS.txt` records why it is reached there rather
   than through a `test/gates.toml` row. -}

import io.{readLines}
import list.{lookup}
import string.{stripSuffix}
import test.{Expectation(..)}
import test_process.{
  disagreeingFloorBlocks,
  missingUnits,
  testAssertionCount,
  underRoot,
  ungradedRosterRows,
  unrosteredUnits,
}

{- | Which engine a module is spawned under, and why.

   The two native reasons stay distinguishable because they drain on
   different events: a `NativeCapability` row drains when the module stops
   statically reaching an extern the interpreter does not bind, a
   `NativeSpeed` row when the interpreter gets fast enough to carry it. A
   single undifferentiated "native" list cannot say which of those to
   re-check.

   `EvalAndNative` is not a third reason to prefer one engine but a pair of
   spawns: the module runs under the interpreter and again under the native
   backend, and the two counts must be EQUAL TO EACH OTHER before either is
   graded against the floor at all. The arm-vs-arm rule is equality rather
   than "both at least the floor" because the arms running different numbers
   of assertions is itself the regression to catch; a rule that only bounds
   them below lets one arm drop while it stays over the floor, and nothing
   says so. The agreed count is then graded `>= floor`, the same way every
   other row is, so adding assertions to a module never fails its row.

   It is what a module takes whenever it can take both, because the eval
   arm alone never typechecks the module the way a native build does — a
   doctest that is not valid Medaka still passes under the interpreter,
   which is how #3291's 91 unbuildable regex examples stayed green. A row
   drains out of `EvalAndNative` the moment the two arms stop agreeing, and
   then by moving to `Eval` with the reason written down, never by dropping
   the second spawn. `async` is the row waiting to drain the other way: it
   is `Eval` today only because its native doctest build fails whole-file
   on two `sleep`-bearing examples (#3300), and it joins `EvalAndNative`
   when that is fixed. -}
data Engine = Eval | NativeCapability | NativeSpeed | EvalAndNative

-- The extra `medaka test` arguments a module is spawned with.
--
-- `EvalAndNative` is two spawns and so has no single argument list;
-- `runModule` matches it before it can reach here, and the arm below
-- exists only because this function must be total.
engineArgs : Engine -> List String
engineArgs Eval = []
engineArgs NativeCapability = ["--native"]
engineArgs NativeSpeed = ["--native"]
engineArgs EvalAndNative = []

-- How a module's engine is named in this gate's own failure message, so a
-- spawn error says which arm produced it.
engineLabel : Engine -> String
engineLabel Eval = "eval"
engineLabel NativeCapability = "native (capability)"
engineLabel NativeSpeed = "native (speed)"
engineLabel EvalAndNative = "eval+native"

{- | The roster: module name, the assertion-count floor it commits, and the
   engine it runs under.

   A floor is the count committed when the row was written, never a
   recomputation: adding tests only raises the real count (`>= floor` still
   passes), while removing them, or a discovery regression, drops below the
   floor and fails. Raise one floor when you add tests to that module; never
   regenerate every floor from a fresh run, which would turn the guard into
   a tautology.

   The floors are `summary.passed` counts, not source-text sums of
   `prop`/`test`/doctest declarations. The two differ: `core` runs 123
   assertions from 29 `prop` declarations and 94 doctest lines, and reading
   the declarations alone commits a floor five times too low.

   `EvalAndNative` is the column every row carries unless something stops
   it, and the two arms must agree before the floor is graded at all. The
   eval arm on its own runs a module's examples without ever building it,
   so a doctest that is not valid Medaka passes there; the native arm is
   what typechecks the generated file, and it is the only arm that can say
   so.

   `NativeCapability` (four rows): the eval arm refuses the module outright
   with `E-PANIC: unbound identifier` on `listDir`, `readFile` or
   `runCommand` — host primitives `medaka test`'s interpreter capability
   policy does not bind — so this is a capability question, not a timing
   one.

   `core` is `Eval` and must stay `Eval`: `medaka test --native
   stdlib/core.mdk` skips its DOCTESTS with a loud named skip citing #1334,
   which `test/diff_compiler_test_native.sh` asserts on. Its 29 `prop`
   declarations do run under the native arm, and only its 94 doctest lines
   are interpreter-only; the row is `Eval` because a native arm that ran 29
   of the module's 123 assertions could not be compared with the eval arm's
   count at all.

   `async` is `Eval` for a reason that is a bug rather than a policy: its
   native doctest build fails whole-file, so the native arm runs 0 of its
   11 assertions rather than some of them. Two examples drive an `Async`
   program with `sleep`, which performs `<Clock>`, and the doctest runner's
   generated top-level binding admits only `<>` (#3300). No annotation
   inside the example fixes an effect-row rejection, so this is not #3291's
   defect wearing a different hat. The row moves to `EvalAndNative` when
   #3300 closes.

   Two rows are worth knowing about when their floor moves. `regex`'s count
   includes two linear-time step-count sentinels that fail loudly if the
   Pike VM's sparse-set dedup or its priority cut is ever lost, so a drop
   there is a complexity regression rather than a bookkeeping one. `hmac`'s
   covers the RFC 4231 vectors and the boundary key lengths, which is what
   makes a reverted variable-key schedule visible at all. -}
suites : List (String, (Int, Engine))
suites = [
  ("args", (44, EvalAndNative)),
  ("array", (53, EvalAndNative)),
  ("async", (10, Eval)),
  -- The RFC 4648 vectors and the canonical-rejection checks. Neither of the
  -- two vehicles named in the header reaches this module, so without this
  -- row nothing would run them.
  ("base32", (28, EvalAndNative)),
  ("base64", (21, EvalAndNative)),
  ("bits64", (31, EvalAndNative)),
  ("bytebuilder", (69, EvalAndNative)),
  ("byteparser", (33, EvalAndNative)),
  -- The `Bytes` surface, including the out-of-range pairing (`get` answers
  -- `None` where `b[i]` panics). Staying out of the COMPILER's import
  -- closure is what keeps this module free of a seed re-mint, and neither
  -- of the two vehicles named in the header reaches it either.
  ("bytes", (87, EvalAndNative)),
  ("core", (123, Eval)),
  ("crypto/hmac", (12, EvalAndNative)),
  ("fs", (8, NativeCapability)),
  ("hash_map", (24, EvalAndNative)),
  ("hash_set", (13, EvalAndNative)),
  ("hex", (23, EvalAndNative)),
  -- The resource-limit examples. Neither of the two vehicles named in the
  -- header reaches this module, so without this row nothing would run them.
  ("http", (6, EvalAndNative)),
  ("io", (8, NativeCapability)),
  ("json", (55, EvalAndNative)),
  ("list", (158, EvalAndNative)),
  ("math", (61, EvalAndNative)),
  ("mut_bytes", (16, EvalAndNative)),
  ("net", (3, EvalAndNative)),
  ("nonempty", (15, EvalAndNative)),
  ("path", (34, EvalAndNative)),
  ("regex", (94, EvalAndNative)),
  ("set", (64, EvalAndNative)),
  -- The assertion library every `*_test.mdk` in the tree imports, whose own
  -- doctests and props importing it never runs. Neither of the two vehicles
  -- named in the header reaches this module either.
  ("test", (72, NativeCapability)),
  ("test_process", (21, NativeCapability)),
  ("time", (36, EvalAndNative)),
  ("toml", (56, EvalAndNative)),
  ("validation", (20, EvalAndNative)),
  ("vector", (32, EvalAndNative)),
]

rosterNames : List String
rosterNames = map fst suites

{- | The `stdlib/` modules that carry no assertion at all today, and the
   engine each is spawned under to prove it.

   A module with nothing to floor cannot take a `suites` row: `>= 0` passes
   for any count, so the row would read as coverage while asserting nothing.
   It takes an `exempt` row instead, derived below from this same list so
   the two cannot drift, plus an exact `== 0` pin. The pin is what keeps the
   exemption from being permanent: the day one of these grows a doctest, its
   pin fails and says to move the row into `suites` with a real floor, which
   is the outcome an unpinned `exempt` row would have let pass silently. -}
zeroAssertionModules : List (String, Engine)
zeroAssertionModules = [
  ("crypto/sha256", Eval),
  ("net_async", Eval),
  ("runtime", Eval),
]

zeroAssertionNames : List String
zeroAssertionNames = map fst zeroAssertionModules

zeroAssertionExempt : List (String, String)
zeroAssertionExempt =
  map
    ((name, _) => (
      name,
      "carries no assertion today; the `stdlib/\{name}.mdk runs 0 assertions` pin below fails the day it grows one",
    ))
    zeroAssertionModules

{- | The `stdlib/*.mdk` modules this gate does not floor, each paired with
   the reason.

   A row is licensed for a module some other enrolled vehicle already runs,
   or for one with nothing to floor. Anything else under `stdlib/` is a
   suite and belongs in `suites` — that is what keeps a new module from
   being added silently, since a module in neither list fails the closure
   check below. The guard after it fails if a row here names a module that
   is not on disk, so a rename cannot leave the roster quietly open. -}
exempt : List (String, String)
exempt =
  [
      (
        "map",
        "already run by `test/diff_compiler_test_native.sh`, whose `MODULES` list spawns it under `--engines eval,native` and grades the same count this roster would",
      ),
      (
        "string",
        "already run by `test/diff_compiler_test_native.sh`, whose `MODULES` list spawns it under `--engines eval,native` and grades the same count this roster would",
      ),
    ]
    ++ zeroAssertionExempt

exemptNames : List String
exemptNames = map fst exempt

{- | Runs one module under its engine and returns its executed-assertion
   count, or an error naming the engine and what went wrong.

   Every constructor is named explicitly and none of the clauses is a bare
   variable. `EvalAndNative` is the one engine that is two spawns, so it
   must reach `runBothEngines` rather than `runOneSpawn`, and a catch-all
   clause makes that dependent on clause ORDER: with the two clauses
   swapped, the variable pattern matches `EvalAndNative` too, every row
   silently runs its eval arm alone, and the file still exits 0 reporting
   every floor met. Exhaustive arms make the same mistake a missing-arm
   compile error instead. -}
runModule : String -> Engine -> <Exec, IO> Result String Int
runModule name EvalAndNative = runBothEngines (underRoot "stdlib/\{name}.mdk")
runModule name Eval = runOneSpawn name Eval
runModule name NativeCapability = runOneSpawn name NativeCapability
runModule name NativeSpeed = runOneSpawn name NativeSpeed

{- | One `medaka test` spawn of `name` under `engine`.

   The engine is named in the error because the arms fail differently: a
   capability refusal from the eval arm and a genuine assertion failure both
   surface as a nonzero exit, and only the label says which arm was asked. -}
runOneSpawn : String -> Engine -> <Exec, IO> Result String Int
runOneSpawn name engine =
  let path = underRoot "stdlib/\{name}.mdk"
  match testAssertionCount path (engineArgs engine)
    Err e => Err "\{path} (\{engineLabel engine}): \{e}"
    Ok n => Ok n

{- | Runs one module twice, once under each engine, and returns the count
   the two arms agree on.

   Two spawns rather than one `--engines eval,native` spawn because that
   form emits only the first engine's object under `--json`, which is the
   only output `testAssertionCount` can grade.

   The two counts must be equal, not merely both at or above the floor.
   Reducing them to the lower one would pass a module whose native arm has
   quietly stopped running some of its assertions, for as long as the
   remainder stays over the floor; equality makes the divergence itself the
   failure, and the message carries both numbers so the next reader knows
   which arm moved. An arm that refuses to run at all is an `Err` naming
   which one, since the two fail for unrelated reasons (the interpreter
   over an unbound extern, the native backend over a doctest that does not
   typecheck). -}
runBothEngines : String -> <Exec, IO> Result String Int
runBothEngines path = match testAssertionCount path []
  Err e => Err "\{path} (eval arm of \{engineLabel EvalAndNative}): \{e}"
  Ok evalRan => match testAssertionCount path ["--native"]
    Err e => Err "\{path} (native arm of \{engineLabel EvalAndNative}): \{e}"
    Ok nativeRan =>
      if evalRan == nativeRan then
        Ok evalRan
      else
        Err
          "\{path} (\{engineLabel EvalAndNative}): the eval arm ran \{intToString evalRan} assertions and the native arm ran \{intToString nativeRan} — the arms must run the same assertions, so one of them has stopped discovering some"

{- | Every rostered module run exactly once, shared by every assertion below
   — top-level nullary bindings are memoized, so a spawn placed here and
   read from two `test` decls executes once, not twice. -}
results : <Exec, IO> List (String, (Int, Result String Int))
results =
  map ((name, (floor, engine)) => (name, (floor, runModule name engine))) suites

{- | Grades one module's floor. The message names the module and both
   numbers on a miss; a run that failed outright reports the spawn's own
   text instead, so a broken assertion inside a module is never mistaken for
   a count that merely fell short. -}
floorExpectation : String -> <Exec, IO> Expectation
floorExpectation name = match lookup name results
  None => Fail "stdlib/\{name}.mdk has no `results` entry" "" ""
  Some (floor, Err e) =>
    Fail e "\{intToString floor} assertions" "spawn/run error"
  Some (floor, Ok ran) =>
    if ran >= floor then
      Pass "\{intToString floor} assertions" "\{intToString ran} assertions"
    else
      Fail
        "stdlib/\{name}.mdk — only \{intToString ran} assertions ran, expected >= \{intToString floor} (vacuous-green guard: discovery may have silently stopped finding tests)"
        "\{intToString floor} assertions"
        "\{intToString ran} assertions"

test "stdlib/args.mdk executed >= 44 assertions" = floorExpectation "args"

test "stdlib/array.mdk executed >= 53 assertions" = floorExpectation "array"

test "stdlib/async.mdk executed >= 10 assertions" = floorExpectation "async"

test "stdlib/base32.mdk executed >= 28 assertions" = floorExpectation "base32"

test "stdlib/base64.mdk executed >= 21 assertions" = floorExpectation "base64"

test "stdlib/bits64.mdk executed >= 31 assertions" = floorExpectation "bits64"

test "stdlib/bytebuilder.mdk executed >= 69 assertions" =
  floorExpectation "bytebuilder"

test "stdlib/byteparser.mdk executed >= 33 assertions" =
  floorExpectation "byteparser"

test "stdlib/bytes.mdk executed >= 87 assertions" = floorExpectation "bytes"

test "stdlib/core.mdk executed >= 123 assertions" = floorExpectation "core"

test "stdlib/crypto/hmac.mdk executed >= 12 assertions" =
  floorExpectation "crypto/hmac"

test "stdlib/fs.mdk executed >= 8 assertions" = floorExpectation "fs"

test "stdlib/hash_map.mdk executed >= 24 assertions" =
  floorExpectation "hash_map"

test "stdlib/hash_set.mdk executed >= 13 assertions" =
  floorExpectation "hash_set"

test "stdlib/hex.mdk executed >= 23 assertions" = floorExpectation "hex"

test "stdlib/http.mdk executed >= 6 assertions" = floorExpectation "http"

test "stdlib/io.mdk executed >= 8 assertions" = floorExpectation "io"

test "stdlib/json.mdk executed >= 55 assertions" = floorExpectation "json"

test "stdlib/list.mdk executed >= 158 assertions" = floorExpectation "list"

test "stdlib/math.mdk executed >= 61 assertions" = floorExpectation "math"

test "stdlib/mut_bytes.mdk executed >= 16 assertions" =
  floorExpectation "mut_bytes"

test "stdlib/net.mdk executed >= 3 assertions" = floorExpectation "net"

test "stdlib/nonempty.mdk executed >= 15 assertions" =
  floorExpectation "nonempty"

test "stdlib/path.mdk executed >= 34 assertions" = floorExpectation "path"

test "stdlib/regex.mdk executed >= 94 assertions" = floorExpectation "regex"

test "stdlib/set.mdk executed >= 64 assertions" = floorExpectation "set"

test "stdlib/test.mdk executed >= 72 assertions" = floorExpectation "test"

test "stdlib/test_process.mdk executed >= 21 assertions" =
  floorExpectation "test_process"

test "stdlib/time.mdk executed >= 36 assertions" = floorExpectation "time"

test "stdlib/toml.mdk executed >= 56 assertions" = floorExpectation "toml"

test "stdlib/validation.mdk executed >= 20 assertions" =
  floorExpectation "validation"

test "stdlib/vector.mdk executed >= 32 assertions" = floorExpectation "vector"

-- # The zero-assertion pins

zeroResults : <Exec, IO> List (String, Result String Int)
zeroResults =
  map ((name, engine) => (name, runModule name engine)) zeroAssertionModules

{- | Grades one exempt-because-empty module: its count must be exactly 0,
   never merely at least 0. A module that has grown an assertion fails here
   with the count to commit as its new floor. -}
zeroExpectation : String -> <Exec, IO> Expectation
zeroExpectation name = match lookup name zeroResults
  None => Fail "stdlib/\{name}.mdk has no `zeroResults` entry" "" ""
  Some (Err e) => Fail e "0 assertions" "spawn/run error"
  Some (Ok ran) =>
    if ran == 0 then
      Pass "0 assertions" "0 assertions"
    else
      Fail
        "stdlib/\{name}.mdk now runs \{intToString ran} assertions, but it is an `exempt` row, so no floor protects them — move it into `suites` with \{intToString ran} as its floor"
        "0 assertions"
        "\{intToString ran} assertions"

test "stdlib/crypto/sha256.mdk runs 0 assertions" =
  zeroExpectation "crypto/sha256"

test "stdlib/net_async.mdk runs 0 assertions" = zeroExpectation "net_async"

test "stdlib/runtime.mdk runs 0 assertions" = zeroExpectation "runtime"

-- # Non-vacuity

ranCountOf : (String, (Int, Result String Int)) -> Int
ranCountOf (_, (_, Ok n)) = n
ranCountOf (_, (_, Err _)) = 0

totalRan : <Exec, IO> Int
totalRan = sum (map ranCountOf results)

test "stdlib in-language suite ran at least one assertion" =
  if totalRan > 0 then
    Pass "> 0 assertions" "\{intToString totalRan} assertions"
  else
    Fail
      "no assertions ran at all across the stdlib in-language suite"
      "> 0 assertions"
      "0 assertions"

{- # Roster closure

   Every `stdlib/*.mdk` must be accounted for: a row in `suites` above, or a
   reasoned row in `exempt`. Without this a new stdlib module is added with
   its assertions run by nothing, which is the state #3214 records and this
   gate exists to close, and nothing anywhere would say so.

   The roster is over the modules themselves, not over `*_test.mdk`
   siblings, so the unit namer is `stripSuffix ".mdk"` rather than
   `test_process.testFileStem`, and the two general helpers
   (`unrosteredUnits`/`missingUnits`) are called directly rather than
   through their `*TestFiles` wrappers. A directory entry that is not a
   Medaka module (`README.md`, the `.lextok.golden` and `.test.golden`
   files) names no unit and is skipped. -}

-- | The module name a `stdlib/` directory entry names, or `None` when the
-- entry is not a Medaka module.
--
-- > moduleStem "set.mdk"
-- Some "set"
-- > moduleStem "set.lextok.golden"
-- None
moduleStem : String -> Option String
moduleStem name = stripSuffix ".mdk" name

-- Every `stdlib/` entry, with a subdirectory replaced by its own entries
-- spelled `dir/name`, so `crypto/hmac.mdk` names the unit `crypto/hmac`.
stdlibEntries : <FileRead, IO> Result String (List String)
stdlibEntries = match listDir (underRoot "stdlib")
  Err e => Err "could not list stdlib: \{e}"
  Ok names => Ok (flatMap subdirEntriesOrSelf names)

subdirEntriesOrSelf : String -> <FileRead, IO> List String
subdirEntriesOrSelf name = match listDir (underRoot "stdlib/\{name}")
  Ok inner => map (n => "\{name}/\{n}") inner
  Err _ => [name]

unrosteredModules : <FileRead, IO> Result String (List String)
unrosteredModules =
  map (unrosteredUnits moduleStem (rosterNames ++ exemptNames)) stdlibEntries

test "every stdlib/*.mdk module has a `suites` or `exempt` row" =
  match unrosteredModules
    Err e => Fail e "no unrostered modules" "listDir failed"
    Ok [] => Pass "no unrostered modules" "no unrostered modules"
    Ok extras =>
      Fail
        "unaccounted stdlib/*.mdk module(s): \{debug extras} — add a row to `suites` in this file with the floor it commits today, or an `exempt` row saying why it is not floored here"
        "no unrostered modules"
        "\{debug extras}"

phantomExemptions : <FileRead, IO> Result String (List String)
phantomExemptions = map (missingUnits moduleStem exemptNames) stdlibEntries

test "every `exempt` row names a module that exists" = match phantomExemptions
  Err e => Fail e "no phantom exemptions" "listDir failed"
  Ok [] => Pass "no phantom exemptions" "no phantom exemptions"
  Ok extras =>
    Fail
      "`exempt` names stdlib module(s) that do not exist: \{debug extras} — a stale exemption re-opens the roster to a module of that name"
      "no phantom exemptions"
      "\{debug extras}"

{- | A module carrying both a `suites` row and an `exempt` row.

   The closure check above reads the two lists concatenated, so it cannot
   see a module in both — it floors the module and simultaneously records a
   reason not to, and whichever of the two is wrong is the one nothing
   reports. Every other check in this file stays green on an overlap. -}
doublyRosteredModules : List String
doublyRosteredModules = filter (n => elem n exemptNames) rosterNames

test "no stdlib module has both a `suites` and an `exempt` row" =
  match doublyRosteredModules
    [] => Pass "no doubly-rostered modules" "no doubly-rostered modules"
    extras =>
      Fail
        "stdlib module(s) with both a `suites` row and an `exempt` row: \{debug extras} — delete whichever is wrong; the closure check reads the two lists joined and cannot see the overlap"
        "no doubly-rostered modules"
        "\{debug extras}"

-- A zero-assertion module is pinned above and exempted below from the same
-- list, so the two cannot disagree; this fails if that derivation is ever
-- replaced by a second hand-written list.
unexemptedZeroRows : List String
unexemptedZeroRows = filter (n => not (elem n exemptNames)) zeroAssertionNames

test "every `zeroAssertionModules` row is also an `exempt` row" =
  match unexemptedZeroRows
    [] => Pass "no unexempted zero rows" "no unexempted zero rows"
    extras =>
      Fail
        "zero-assertion module(s) with no `exempt` row: \{debug extras} — the roster closure check would report them unaccounted"
        "no unexempted zero rows"
        "\{debug extras}"

{- # Roster/assertion lockstep

   `results` is a shared memoized binding computed for every `suites` row
   whether or not anything asserts on it: a row with no `test` block of its
   own is still spawned, and its verdict is silently discarded. The closure
   checks above prove only that a MODULE is named somewhere; they say
   nothing about whether an assertion grades it. `test_process`'s
   `ungradedRosterRows` closes that gap by reading this file's own source
   text rather than hand-maintaining a second list beside the `test` blocks,
   which would relocate the bug from "no check" to "two lists that can
   drift" (#2997). -}

floorCallOpen : String
floorCallOpen = "floorExpectation \""

ownSourceLines : <IO> Result String (List String)
ownSourceLines = readLines (underRoot "test/stdlib_suite_test.mdk")

uncheckedRows : <IO> Result String (List String)
uncheckedRows =
  map
    (ls => ungradedRosterRows "stdlib/" floorCallOpen rosterNames ls)
    ownSourceLines

test "every `suites` row has a matching `test` block" = match uncheckedRows
  Err e => Fail e "no unchecked rows" "read failed"
  Ok [] => Pass "no unchecked rows" "no unchecked rows"
  Ok extras =>
    Fail
      "suites row(s) with no `test \"stdlib/<name>.mdk executed >= …\"` block grading that same name: \{debug extras} — `results` is computed for them but never turned into an assertion (#2997)"
      "no unchecked rows"
      "\{debug extras}"

disagreeingBlocks : <IO> Result String (List String)
disagreeingBlocks =
  map (ls => disagreeingFloorBlocks "stdlib/" floorCallOpen ls) ownSourceLines

test "every floor assertion grades the module its own title names" =
  match disagreeingBlocks
    Err e => Fail e "no title/argument disagreements" "read failed"
    Ok [] =>
      Pass "no title/argument disagreements" "no title/argument disagreements"
    Ok bad =>
      Fail
        "floor assertion block(s) whose title and `floorExpectation` argument name different modules: \{debug bad} — the titled row's floor is never applied and the graded row's is applied twice, while the row reads as covered"
        "no title/argument disagreements"
        "\{debug bad}"
